    id: "c389f782bde87f198322",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "One year on, Gaza mourns journalist Anas al-Sharif’s death",
    summary: "Gaza remembers Anas al-Sharif, a journalist whose voice defined the impact of war on civilian lives.",
    preview: "In Pictures One year on, Gaza mourns journalist Anas al-Sharif’s death Gaza remembers Anas al-Sharif, a journalist whose voice defined the impact of war on civilian lives. On the first anniversary of the killing of Al Jazeera correspondent Anas al-Sharif in Gaza, Palestinians and press freedom advocates are remembering a journalist who became one of the defining voices of Israel’s war on the enclave.",
    body: "In Pictures\n\nOne year on, Gaza mourns journalist Anas al-Sharif’s death\n\nGaza remembers Anas al-Sharif, a journalist whose voice defined the impact of war on civilian lives.\n\nOn the first anniversary of the killing of Al Jazeera correspondent Anas al-Sharif in Gaza, Palestinians and press freedom advocates are remembering a journalist who became one of the defining voices of Israel’s war on the enclave.\n\nBorn in 1996 in the Jabalya refugee camp, al-Sharif rose from a local cameraman to a widely recognised face across the Arab world through his relentless reports on Israel’s bombardment and its toll on civilians.\n\nHe was killed in an Israeli air strike on a clearly marked press tent near Gaza City’s al-Shifa Hospital, in an attack that also claimed the lives of Al Jazeera staff members Mohammed Qreiqeh, cameraman Ibrahim Zahir and producer Mohammed Noufal, and drew condemnation from UN experts and rights groups.\n\nHis death, along with that of other Gaza journalists, has been cited as part of a broader pattern of targeting media workers.\n\nIn a will recorded before his killing, al-Sharif urged people not to forget Gaza and to remain steadfast in defending Palestine — words that continue to be shared widely online and echoed at memorials marking one year since his death.",
